# Update README.md with the change

When you change how Foodie is installed, started, configured, or used, update `README.md` in the same work.

Update it when any of these happen:

- New or changed install/run commands (Python, npm, MySQL, migrate, Vite, Daphne)
- New environment variables or `.env.example` keys
- A new Django migration operators must run after pull
- Admin/employee creation or `/db` setup flow changes
- Login URLs, proxy ports, or WebSocket startup requirements change
- A user-facing workflow in the README notes section becomes wrong (kitchen sync, table vacate, protein field, cart lifecycle)

Do not:

- Put real passwords, `DB_SETUP_SECRET`, or `.env` values in the README
- Recommend `createsuperuser` as the way to create the first Foodie admin (use `/db` Create First Admin)
- Add a testing phase or automated-test instructions unless the user asked for them

Keep the README short. Change the existing sections instead of appending duplicate “latest change” dumps.
